+<body>
+<header><div class="wrap">
+  <a class="home" href="index.html">&lsaquo; Back to agilebykillette.com</a>
+  <div class="eyebrow">Experience by Agile by Killette</div>
+  <h1>Backup RTE: <span class="g">When the Train Needs a Second Set of Hands</span></h1>
+  <div class="lede">Backing up a Release Train Engineer is not holding the seat warm. It is making sure nobody can tell the seat changed hands &mdash; that PI Planning still lands, that every risk gets ROAMed, and that the impediments teams cannot clear still get cleared.</div>
+  <div class="tagline">Serving the ART. Elevating the outcome.</div>
+</div></header>
+<div class="wrap">
+<figure class="heroshot tall"><img src="hero-backup-rte.jpg" alt="Backup RTE one-pager &mdash; nine capabilities across PI Planning, risk, the confidence vote, dependencies, impediments, and leadership coaching"><figcaption>The whole role on one page.</figcaption></figure>
+
+<p class="intro">I have served as Backup RTE on an eight-team train. This is what the role actually covered &mdash; not the job description version, but the work that had to happen every PI for the train to keep moving.</p>
+
+<section class="body">
+
+<h2 class="sec">The train I backed up</h2>
+<div class="statrow">
+  <div class="stat"><b>8</b><i></i><span>Teams on the train</span></div>
+  <div class="stat"><b>~72</b><i></i><span>People aligned each PI</span></div>
+  <div class="stat"><b>~25</b><i></i><span>Impediments cleared per PI</span></div>
+</div>
+<p class="lead">Eight teams, roughly seventy-two people, and about twenty-five impediments to clear every increment. At that size nothing coasts. The train either has someone holding the seams together or it does not.</p>
+
+<h2 class="sec">What the role covered</h2>
+<div class="capgrid">
+<div class="cap"><h4>PI Planning Facilitation</h4><ul><li>Sized the event to the lift of the PI &mdash; a full week when the scope earned it, three days when it didn&rsquo;t.</li><li>Sent all eight teams out aligned, clear, and committed &mdash; not just scheduled.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Risk &amp; ROAM</h4><ul><li>Surfaced risks in the open and ROAMed every one &mdash; resolved, owned, accepted, or mitigated.</li><li>Pushed mitigation during the event, so teams committed with risks worked, not just logged.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Confidence Vote</h4><ul><li>Ran the vote so leadership heard, in the room, whether teams expected a good PI or a hard one.</li><li>Turned a low vote into commitments from leadership on the concerns raised &mdash; not just a replan.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Roadmap Planning</h4><ul><li>Partnered with Product on roadmaps teams could actually commit to.</li><li>Tied each PI to business objectives, not just available capacity.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>MVP of Epics</h4><ul><li>Helped Product cut epics to the smallest slice that proves value.</li><li>Less risk carried, and value in customers&rsquo; hands sooner.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Dependency Mapping</h4><ul><li>Mapped dependencies across the ART so surprises surfaced in planning.</li><li>Coached individual teams to see, own, and manage their own.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>ART Dependency Sessions</h4><ul><li>Facilitated cross-team dependency sessions as often as once a week.</li><li>Turned blockers into conversations before they turned into delays.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Impediments &amp; Escalation</h4><ul><li>Escalated what teams couldn&rsquo;t clear alone and tracked roughly 25 a PI to closure, status visible to everyone.</li><li>Removed obstacles, kept value flowing, and made follow-through the norm rather than the exception.</li></ul></div>
+<div class="cap"><h4>Leadership &amp; Culture Coaching</h4><ul><li>Coached leaders on ART operations and the mindset behind them.</li><li>Built trust, collaboration, and a real habit of continuous improvement.</li></ul></div>
+</div>
+
+<h2 class="sec">Why the confidence vote matters most</h2>
+<p class="lead">Of everything on that list, the confidence vote is the one I protect hardest &mdash; and the one most often treated as a formality at the end of a long two days.</p>
+<p class="lead">That vote tells a story nothing else in the event tells. Leadership hears, straight from the teams and in their own hands, whether this PI looks like a win or a grind. No status report carries that. No summary deck survives contact with it.</p>
+<p class="lead">When the vote comes in low, the instinct is to send everyone back to replan. That is the wrong instinct. A low vote is the one moment leadership is <b>in the room, listening, and able to commit</b> to the concerns being raised. If leadership has answers, that is when the team should get them &mdash; not two weeks later in an escalation thread.</p>
+<div class="pull"><div class="q">"Risks get ROAMed. Dependencies get mapped. But the vote is where the train tells the truth."</div></div>
+
+<h2 class="sec">The goal is simple</h2>
+<div class="seq"><span>REMOVE OBSTACLES</span><i>&rarr;</i><span>ALIGN TEAMS</span><i>&rarr;</i><span>DELIVER VALUE</span></div>
+<p class="lead">I serve the ART so the ART can serve the customer. People first, relentless collaboration, transparency always, and a real habit of continuous improvement &mdash; not as slogans on a wall, but as the four things that decide whether an eight-team train arrives.</p>
+<div class="callout"><b>What backup actually means.</b> The measure of the role is not what happens while the RTE is out. It is what the teams notice &mdash; which should be nothing at all.</div>
+
+<p class="lead"><a class="onepager" href="backup-rte-onepager.png" download>&darr;&nbsp; Download the one-page version</a></p>
+
+</section>
+
+<div class="final">
+  <h2>Need coverage for your ART?</h2>
+  <p>Whether it is backup for an RTE who is out, or a fractional RTE a few days a month, the job is the same: keep the train on track, leaders informed, and teams maturing.</p>
+  <a class="btn" href="mailto:wendell@agilebykillette.com?subject=Agile%20by%20Killette%20-%20Backup%20RTE">Book a conversation &rarr;</a>
+</div>
+
+</div>
